---
description: Project-wide internal BIGINT keys and random UUID public identifiers
alwaysApply: true
---

# Record identifiers

- Applies to the entire project: database, ORM, API, UI, integrations, exports, seeds, migrations, tests and documentation.
- Every application-owned table, including technical tables, has two identifiers:
  - `id BIGINT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY`: internal identity only.
  - `public_id UUID NOT NULL DEFAULT pg_catalog.gen_random_uuid() UNIQUE`: random UUIDv4 for public references.
- All relational foreign keys and SQL joins use internal BIGINT `id`; never use `public_id` as a relational FK.
- Generate public IDs on the trusted server/database. Do not derive them from numeric IDs, row order, timestamps or public source values; do not use predictable UUID versions or browser-generated IDs for ordinary creates.
- `public_id` is immutable after creation, including imports that preserve timestamps. Never regenerate it during edits, synchronization, restart, retry, export or re-seeding.
- API route params, query/body references, browser URLs, client state, links and frontend exports use canonical lowercase UUIDv4 strings. Reject decimal internal IDs at public endpoints; do not fall back to numeric lookup.
- Keep existing DTO field names when helpful: `id = row.public_id`, `projectId = project.public_id`. These are public strings, not storage keys.
- Resolve a supplied UUID to the authorized row server-side, then use its numeric key for relations and transactions. Never trust client owner/assignee IDs to change ownership.
- Use explicit response DTOs, including nested objects; never spread ORM rows or serialize internal numeric PK/FK values, internal-ID cursors or SQL diagnostics to the frontend.
- Pagination may order by internal IDs, but tokens must be opaque and must not reveal decodable numeric keys; expose only public IDs in client-visible references.
- UUID complexity does not replace owner authentication or object-level authorization. Check permissions for every read, mutation, import and export; a leaked UUID must not grant access.
- Provider project/issue IDs remain separate external source values; never substitute them for local numeric or public UUID identities. Polymorphic historical references require an explicit migration/serialization policy.
- Controlled imports may supply reviewed UUIDv4 values. Persist the source-scoped old ID → internal ID + public UUID mapping and reuse it for reproducible conversions; never regenerate UUIDs on reruns.
- Live and seed mappings have distinct provenance. Preserve existing valid public UUIDs and resolve/reject legacy CUID URLs only through reviewed compatibility mapping, never by guessing identities.
- Retain PostgreSQL comments on every table and column. Test UUID generation, uniqueness, nullability, immutability, numeric FKs, public-ID validation and authorization/DTO leakage at the API boundary.
- Current transition: SQL target implements this standard; active Prisma/API still uses legacy CUIDs until compatible #33 adoption. Do not point the old application at the numeric target or claim UUID-only public APIs are deployed before integration.

Example target DTO: `{ id: project.public_id, companyId: project.company.public_id }`.
Example storage relation: `work_logs.work_item_id BIGINT REFERENCES work_items(id)`.
